<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Optimal estimation of a factorizable density
using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks<span class="ltx_pubnotes"><span class="ltx_pubnotes_content"><span class="ltx_pubnote ltx_role_thanks"><span class="ltx_note_name">Thanks: </span>The research was supported by ONR Grants N00014-25-1-2317 and N00014-25-1-2317, and the NSF Grant DMS-2210833.</span></span></span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jianqing Fan
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Operations Research and Financial Engineering, Princeton University, jqfan@princeton.edu</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yihong Gu
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Biomedical Informatics, Harvard University,
yihong_gu@hms.harvard.edu</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ximing Li
</span><span id="id3" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_note ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Department of Operations Research and Financial Engineering, Princeton University, xl7408@princeton.edu</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper investigates the score-based diffusion models for density estimation when the target density admits a factorizable low-dimensional nonparametric structure. To be specific, we show that when the log density admits a <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-way interaction model with <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth components, the vanilla diffusion model, which uses a fully connected ReLU neural network for score matching, can attain optimal <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n^{-\beta/(2\beta+d^{*})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-\beta/(2\beta+d^{*})}</annotation></semantics></math> statistical rate of convergence in total variation distance. This is, to the best of our knowledge, the first in the literature showing that diffusion models with standard configurations can adapt to the low-dimensional factorizable structures. The main challenge is that the low-dimensional factorizable structure no longer holds for most of the diffused timesteps, and it is very challenging to show that these diffused score functions can be well approximated without a significant increase in the number of network parameters. Our key insight is to demonstrate that the diffused score functions can be decomposed into a composition of either super-smooth or low-dimensional components, leading to a new approximation error analysis of ReLU neural networks with respect to the diffused score function. The rate of convergence under the 1-Wasserstein distance is also derived with a slight modification of the method.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The recent years have witnessed the remarkable success of diffusion models in generating images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon,, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al.,, 2020</a>)</cite>,
audios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al.,, 2020</a>)</cite>, and videos <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al.,, 2022</a>)</cite>. See <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., (2024)</a></cite> for an overview of this subject. Their workflow is two‑stage: a forward diffusion progressively corrupts the data with noise, which creates a map from the data distribution to a known noise distribution (usually Gaussian), and a backward process reconstructs the data distribution by solving the time‑reversal stochastic differential equations (SDEs), which provides a method that computes the inverse map from the noise distribution to the data distribution. The backward SDE requires the diffused score function – the gradient of the log-density along the noise path – which is learned via score matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vincent,, 2011</a>)</cite> with neural networks. Substituting this estimated score into the reverse SDE then transforms pure Gaussian noise into samples whose distribution is close to the target distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite their empirical success, theoretical understanding is sparse. Though estimating the generic high-dimensional distributions is fundamentally difficult and will suffer from the curse of dimensionality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khas’minskii,, 1979</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stone,, 1982</a>)</cite>, high-dimensional data in the real world often admits a low-dimensional structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidt-Hieber,, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al.,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sclocchi et al.,, 2025</a>)</cite>. This paper contributes to understanding how diffusion models with a standard neural network approximation to the score function can efficiently adapt to the unknown intrinsic low-dimensional structures. Focusing on densities that factor into lower‑dimensional components, we prove no curse-of-dimensionality convergence rates in both total variation (TV) and 1‑Wasserstein distance and establish the adaptive minimax optimality under total variation for diffusion models blind to any knowledge of function forms. This answers an important and challenging open problem on the adaptive learning of a diffusion model to local dimensional density structure using the standard neural network architecture.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Diffusion models and the problem under study</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical foundation for the diffusion models lies in the literature on the time-reversal of stochastic differential equations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson,, 1982</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haussmann and Pardoux,, 1986</a>)</cite>. Let <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the target distribution. Consider an Ornstein-Uhlenbeck (OU) process as the forward process</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}X_{t}=-\beta_{t}X_{t}\mathrm{~d}t+\sqrt{2\beta_{t}}\mathrm{~d}B_{t},\qquad X_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}X_{t}=-\beta_{t}X_{t}\mathrm{~d}t+\sqrt{2\beta_{t}}\mathrm{~d}B_{t},\qquad X_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(B_{t}\right)_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(B_{t}\right)_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard Brownian motion and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math> is user-chosen weighting function. Denote the marginal density of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. For a fixed time endpoint <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math> specified later, it is well-known that the backward process <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Y_{t}\right)_{[0,\overline{T}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Y_{t}\right)_{[0,\overline{T}]}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{t}=X_{\overline{T}-\mathrm{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{t}=X_{\overline{T}-\mathrm{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution to the following SDE:</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle dY_{t}=\beta_{\overline{T}-t}\left(Y_{t}+2\nabla\log p_{\overline{T}-t}\left(Y_{t}\right)\right)dt+\sqrt{2\beta_{\overline{T}-t}}dZ_{t},\qquad Y_{0}\sim p_{\overline{T}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle dY_{t}=\beta_{\overline{T}-t}\left(Y_{t}+2\nabla\log p_{\overline{T}-t}\left(Y_{t}\right)\right)dt+\sqrt{2\beta_{\overline{T}-t}}dZ_{t},\qquad Y_{0}\sim p_{\overline{T}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(Z_{t}\right)_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(Z_{t}\right)_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> is another standard Brownian motion and <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is referred to as the score function for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Because the OU process rapidly drives <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\overline{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\overline{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> toward the standard Gaussian as <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bakry et al.,, 2014</a>)</cite>, initializing <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from Gaussian noise and running model (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) yields a sample exactly from <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> – provided the score is known. In practice, one discretizes the SDE and learns an estimated <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> from data, thereby obtaining an implementable diffusion model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">diffusion model</em>, motivated by the above SDE, uses the following procedure to estimate the target distribution implicitly. We first specify the forward process (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) that determines how the target <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is transformed to Gaussian noise. Given <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> observations sampled from <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can apply the score-matching to estimate the score functions <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(x,t)=\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(x,t)=\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, typically using neural networks. Finally, plugging in the estimated score function <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> into the reverse process (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) allows it to generate samples <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> whose distribution is close to <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from the noise. For an overview, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Zhao, (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There is a considerable literature on offering statistical analyses of the aforementioned diffusion model, assuming <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to a certain function class; see, for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite>. Focusing on diffusion model using fully connected neural networks for score function estimation, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite> first shows it can attain minimax optimal error rate both in TV and 1-Wasserstein distance when <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to Hölder class. The key idea is that neural networks can approximate the diffused score function in a nearly optimal way under this scenario. Given the key part in the paradigm is to estimate the (diffused) score function from data, there is also a considerable literature focusing on understanding the optimal <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> estimation error of the score function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wibisono et al.,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al.,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dou et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite> with more traditional nonparametric techniques like the kernel density estimator. However, all these convergence rates suffer from the curse of dimensionality that results in an extremely slow rate when the dimension <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is large.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Real‑world data – ranging from physical laws <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dahmen,, 2022</a>)</cite> to images and language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Partee,, 1984</a>)</cite> – often exhibit certain low-dimensional compositional structures. Neural networks are adept at exploiting this and can algorithmically learn low‑dimensional compositional structures without assuming explicit function form <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bauer and Kohler,, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidt-Hieber,, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohler and Langer,, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan and Gu,, 2024</a>)</cite>.
Diffusion models appear to share the same strength: empirical studies indicate they capture the low-dimensional patterns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sclocchi et al.,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sclocchi et al.,, 2025</a>)</cite>, and recent theoretical studies show that the backward sampling dynamic (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) is provably adaptive to low-dimensionality in a structure-agnostic way <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Potaptchik et al.,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Yan, 2024a, </a>)</cite> in terms of discretization error for solving (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) with a known score function. From a statistical viewpoint, however, key questions remain unanswered: It still lacks a clear understanding of whether the entire diffusion model pipeline, including estimating the score function, is sample efficient under the unknown low-dimensional structure. In particular, the sample efficiency of the score‑function estimation stage has not been fully characterized. Although there is some work showing diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Yang,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azangulov et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite> can adapt to the unknown manifold, these are essentially similar to the Hölder case studied by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite>, where the density can be expanded as a series of fixed local polynomial bases and minimax optimal rate can be attained via a variant of kernel density estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Yang,, 2023</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we study whether diffusion models can efficiently estimate distributions from the exponential‑interaction family, a subclass of the general hierarchical composition structure. Although this family exhibits a seemingly simple low‑dimensional structure, it falls far beyond the settings explored by current diffusion model theory, and by taking interaction order sufficiently large, it can include most of the frequently used low-dimensional models. However, in the statistical machine learning community, there is still no practical and sample-efficient estimator for such a density, along with a corresponding algorithm to generate the data from the estimated density.
To be specific, let <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> be the ambient dimension and suppose we observe i.i.d. samples <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from an unknown density <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> that admits the form</p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(x)=\exp\left(\sum_{J\in\mathcal{S}}f_{J}(x_{J})\right)\qquad\text{with}\qquad\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{J\subseteq\{1,\ldots,d\}:|J|=d^{*}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext>with</mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(x)=\exp\left(\sum_{J\in\mathcal{S}}f_{J}(x_{J})\right)\qquad\text{with}\qquad\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{J\subseteq\{1,\ldots,d\}:|J|=d^{*}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{J}=(x_{j})_{j\in J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{J}=(x_{j})_{j\in J}</annotation></semantics></math> and each <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{J}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-variate <math id="S1.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-Hölder function. Our central question is:</p>
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Can diffusion models with fully connected neural networks as score function estimators circumvent the curse of dimensionality under (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) in a structure-agnostic manner?</span></p>
<p id="S1.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">This question is very technically challenging, as the score functions at most time points do not admit a low-dimensional structure. Dedicated efforts are made to answer this critical but challenging question in generative AI.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The density structure (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) depicts the functional form of the distributions that appear in many graphical model frameworks, such as Markov random fields and Bayesian networks. These models succinctly encode local dependencies in high‑dimensional data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Besag,, 1974</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cross and Jain,, 1983</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saul and Jordan,, 1994</a>)</cite> and are therefore widely adopted in modern statistical practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wainwright et al.,, 2008</a>)</cite>. Despite this popularity in statistical modeling, it still lacks an estimator that is both theoretically minimax‑optimal and easily implementable. Existing guarantees are largely confined to special cases such as tree‑structured graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al.,, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Györfi et al.,, 2023</a>)</cite>. In more general settings, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vandermeulen et al., 2024a ()</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vandermeulen et al., 2024b ()</a></cite> proposes explicit density estimators <math id="S1.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, but sampling from them is computationally expensive, and the convergence rate is not optimal. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., (2025)</a></cite> brings diffusion models into the picture, but they adopted their specially designed neural network that essentially performs integration, which is impractical in implementation. In short, there is still no simultaneously practical and provably sample‑efficient procedure for learning and generating samples from densities of the form (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike previous manifold settings that are either linear <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al.,, 2023</a>)</cite> or nonparametric <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Yang,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azangulov et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite> whose low-dimensional structure of similar form is maintained throughout all the timesteps <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the main challenge here is that this low-dimensional factorization structure no longer holds for diffused <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is not very small.
From a technical perspective, even if the interaction set <math id="S1.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> were known, one cannot easily construct a fixed set of basis functions that can approximate densities of the form (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>) with a dimension‑independent error, indicating that one cannot get rid of the curse of dimensionality via previous proof strategies in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Yang, (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azangulov et al., (2024)</a></cite>.
Addressing this challenge calls for new insights and tools, which are also essential for the understanding of diffusion models under the general hierarchical composition structures.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper answers the question in the affirmative. To be specific, we show that under the general regularity condition that the density <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on a unit cube and is bounded from below and above, the diffusion model can obtain the error rate <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{-\frac{\beta}{2\beta+d^{*}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-\frac{\beta}{2\beta+d^{*}}}</annotation></semantics></math> in TV distance if <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> admits the low-dimensional factorized structure (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>). The diffusion model we analyzed employs a standard fully connected ReLU neural network that estimates the score function for all <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, without any prior knowledge of the underlying factorization. This matches the minimax optimal convergence rate in TV distance when <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> lies within this exponential interaction family and has implications both in diffusion model theory and structured density estimation and sampling.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We prove that a vanilla diffusion model – equipped with a fully connected ReLU network for score estimation – adaptively exploits the low‑dimensional factorization in (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>). This composition of a super‑smooth link and an interaction term departs sharply from previously analyzed settings and unveils, for the first time, how diffusion models learn such a hierarchical structure.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Simulating the reverse SDE to TV distance <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> only requires <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\mathrm{poly}(\log(d),d^{*})\cdot\epsilon^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\mathrm{poly}(\log(d),d^{*})\cdot\epsilon^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Yan, 2024a, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. Coupled with our statistically minimax-optimal rate of convergence, this makes the vanilla diffusion model, to the best of our knowledge, the first method that is simultaneously provably optimal and readily implementable for sampling from the general nonparametric family in (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, following the idea of piecewise score estimator in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite>, we show that one can also attain estimation error <math id="S1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{-\frac{\beta+d^{*}/d}{2\beta+d^{*}}}=o(n^{-\frac{\beta}{2\beta+d^{*}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-\frac{\beta+d^{*}/d}{2\beta+d^{*}}}=o(n^{-\frac{\beta}{2\beta+d^{*}}})</annotation></semantics></math> under 1-Wasserstein distance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The main technical novelty is to derive the dimension-free approximation error of the score function <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(x)=\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(x)=\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> across <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Unlike the previous analysis, which utilizes the fact that <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> itself can be decomposed into a series of bases with an optimal approximation error, we instead decompose the diffused score <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> into a composition of either super-smooth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan,, 1991</a>)</cite> or low-dimensional functions throughout <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Formally, let <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{c}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖼</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{c}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> be any fixed large constant. We show that, for any given depth <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a neural network <math id="S1.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall t\in[(WL)^{-\mathsf{c}},\mathsf{c}\cdot\log(WL)]\qquad\left[\int_{x\in\mathds{R}^{d}}\left\lVert\tilde{s}(x,t)-s_{t}(x)\right\rVert_{2}^{2}p_{t}(x)dx\right]^{1/2}\lesssim\frac{(WL)^{-2\beta/d^{*}}}{\sqrt{t}\wedge 1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝖼</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝖼</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≲</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt><mo>∧</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall t\in[(WL)^{-\mathsf{c}},\mathsf{c}\cdot\log(WL)]\qquad\left[\int_{x\in\mathds{R}^{d}}\left\lVert\tilde{s}(x,t)-s_{t}(x)\right\rVert_{2}^{2}p_{t}(x)dx\right]^{1/2}\lesssim\frac{(WL)^{-2\beta/d^{*}}}{\sqrt{t}\wedge 1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Besides circumventing the curse of dimensionality, our approximation error and stochastic error analyses sharpen prior results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al.,, 2025</a>)</cite> even when <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Particularly, we get rid of the boundary super‑smoothness assumption, require only density bounded from below and above, and obtain explicit error rates for arbitrary network widths and depths without imposing sparse weights. Meanwhile, we establish a stochastic error bound when the weights of neural networks are unbounded or grow exponentially with <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, while the previous stochastic analysis inevitably requires polynomially bounded weights to control the <math id="S1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> cover number.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Related works and comparisons</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Underlying the success of modern deep learning models is the approximation capability of neural networks. Classical universal-approximation results show that a one-hidden-layer neural network can approximate any continuous function on a compact domain to arbitrary precision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hornik,, 1991</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barron,, 1993</a>)</cite>. Yet universality alone is not distinctive – splines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Friedman,, 1991</a>)</cite>, wavelets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Donoho and Johnstone,, 1995</a>)</cite>, and other non-parametric techniques enjoy the same property. Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Telgarsky, (2016)</a></cite> illustrated the benefits of deep neural networks over shallow ones. Based on the the idea of approximating the local polynomial expansion of the target function and the fact that polynomial functions can be efficiently approximated by the constructed neural networks in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Telgarsky, (2016)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky, (2017)</a></cite>, there is a considerable literature on quantifying how the <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> error of a fully connected ReLU network scales with its depth <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> when the target functions lies within some specific function classes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yarotsky,, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohler and Langer,, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al.,, 2021</a>)</cite>. However, these rates suffer from the curse of dimensionality in that they will be slow even for moderate <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidt-Hieber, (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohler and Langer, (2021)</a></cite> show that neural networks can circumvent the curse of dimensionality when the target function is a composition of simple functions. And there is also some literature characterizing the provable advantages of neural networks over traditional nonparametric techniques via establishing the lower bounds for the latter, like wavelets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidt-Hieber,, 2020</a>)</cite> and general linear estimators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Suzuki and Nitanda,, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Imaizumi and Fukumizu,, 2022</a>)</cite>. In the context of the diffusion model, where the function of interest is the diffused score function <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}(x,t)=\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}(x,t)=\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite> developed the approximation ability of neural networks to the diffused score function using the fact that the target density <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by a sequence of fixed basis functions at the optimal rate – a strategy later extended to unknown manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Yang,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azangulov et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, one cannot simply apply similar arguments to the factorized structure we studied.
We propose a novel decomposition of the diffused score function, showing that a low-dimensional compositional structure remains in <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> even when <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is not very small, the regime where the factorized structure breaks down; see the discussion in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Score approximation error ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is a considerable literature on the theoretical understanding of diffusion models. When an accurate score function is provided, reverse-time SDEs generate samples that are close to the target distribution in polynomial time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Bortoli,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Yan, 2024b, </a>)</cite>, and the iteration complexity remains adaptive to the underlying low-dimensional structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li and Yan, 2024a, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. In the statistical setting where the score should be learned from data, diffusion models trained by score matching achieve minimax-optimal rates for Hölder-smooth densities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al.,, 2023</a>)</cite>, for data supported on unknown manifolds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Yang,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Azangulov et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>, and for certain parametric families <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mei and Wu,, 2025</a>)</cite>. Because classical approaches like kernel methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Yang,, 2023</a>)</cite> attain the same optimal rates in these regimes, the distinct advantage of diffusion models remains ambiguous. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cole and Lu, (2024)</a></cite> showed that a vanilla diffusion model can break the curse of dimensionality in a structure-agnostic way, but their bounds require the log-density to be nearly Gaussian and fail to attain optimal rates. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., (2025)</a></cite> considered the exponential-interaction family studied here, but leveraging an impractical, hand-crafted neural architecture that integrates the density – an architecture substantially different from the neural networks commonly used in practice. Therefore, it is still unclear whether the vanilla diffusion model adopted in practice can learn low-dimensional composition structures that neural networks are adept at. This paper makes progress in this direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond diffusion models, deep generative alternatives – generative adversarial networks (GANs), variational autoencoders (VAEs), and normalizing flows – have been analyzed for their statistical efficiency. Recent studies (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang,, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al.,, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Uppal et al.,, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al.,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al.,, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stéphanovitch et al.,, 2024</a></cite>) provide finite-sample guarantees, but almost all assume the target density belongs to a function class where classical methods can also obtain the optimal rate. Another line of work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bos and Schmidt-Hieber,, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vandermeulen et al., 2024a, </a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vandermeulen et al., 2024b, </a>)</cite> proposes new algorithms that can obtain rates that beat the classical curse-of-dimensionality bound <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n^{-\beta/(2\beta+d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-\beta/(2\beta+d)}</annotation></semantics></math> under (<a href="#S1.E3" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>). However, these rates are still sub-optimal, and the resulting estimators do not furnish a practical procedure for drawing samples – two limitations that the vanilla diffusion model we analyzed here overcomes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4 </span>Organization</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of the paper is as follows.
In Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we introduce the setup, in particular, the diffusion model and score-matching.
In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we first provide the approximation error for the score function using a deep fully-connected ReLU network with any architecture hyperparameters.
Based on this generic approximation result, we establish the convergence rate for the distribution generated by the diffusion model (with optimally tuned network parameters).
Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> briefly sketches the proof for the main results and presents our novel technical tools for
deep score approximation.
Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> provides the modification of the diffusion model and the corresponding estimation rate in <math id="S1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance.
All detailed proofs are contained in the supplemental material and are available upon request.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.5 </span>Notations</h3>

<div id="S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following notations will be used throughout this paper. We use <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1},c_{2},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1},c_{2},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the global constants that appear in the statement of any theorem, proposition, corollary, and lemma. We use <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the local intermediate constants in the proof. Hence, all the <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1},c_{2},\cdots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1},c_{2},\cdots</annotation></semantics></math> have unique referred numbers, while all the <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2},\ldots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2},\ldots</annotation></semantics></math> will have different referred numbers in respective proofs.
The notation <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C(A_{1},A_{2},\cdots,A_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(A_{1},A_{2},\cdots,A_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> means that the constant <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> only relies on <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{1},A_{2},\cdots,A_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{1},A_{2},\cdots,A_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math>
We use <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(n)\lesssim b(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(n)\lesssim b(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(n)\gtrsim a(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(n)\gtrsim a(n)</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(n)=O(b(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(n)=O(b(n))</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists some constant <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(n)\leq Cb(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(n)\leq Cb(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, We use <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(b(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(b(n))</annotation></semantics></math> to hidden poly-log factors. Denote <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(n)\asymp b(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(n)\asymp b(n)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(n)\lesssim b(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(n)\lesssim b(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(n)\gtrsim b(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(n)\gtrsim b(n)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a vector <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert x\right\rVert_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert x\right\rVert_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm of <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;p\leq+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;p\leq+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, and
the <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm of <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> will be abbreviated as <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lVert x\right\rVert" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lVert x\right\rVert</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathds{N}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathds{N}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[m]</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the set <math id="S1.SS5.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\cdots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\cdots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Setup and methodology</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the following implicit distribution estimation problem. Let <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the unknown <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-variate density function, and suppose we observe <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimension samples <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\ </annotation></semantics></math> drawn from <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The goal of implicit distribution estimation is to find a transform <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}</annotation></semantics></math> of the noise <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> such that the distribution <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}=\hat{h}(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}=\hat{h}(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is close to <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that when <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is the uniform distribution, <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}</annotation></semantics></math> estimate the inverse cumulative distribution function of <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}=p_{\widehat{X}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}=p_{\widehat{X}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> be the density function of the random variable <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on the data (with randomness <math id="S2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>). One can use the TV distance between <math id="S2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathsf{TV}(\hat{p},p_{0})=\int|\hat{p}(x)-p_{0}(x)|dx," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathsf{TV}(\hat{p},p_{0})=\int|\hat{p}(x)-p_{0}(x)|dx,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">to evaluate the performance of the implicit distribution estimator <math id="S2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{p}_{\widehat{X}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{p}_{\widehat{X}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> or the quality of the simulated sample <math id="S2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> from the target distribution <math id="S2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first introduce some notations used in the construction of the diffusion model estimator. Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}:\mathds{R}^{+}\to\mathds{R}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}:\mathds{R}^{+}\to\mathds{R}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> be a pre-determined weighting function. It is easy to see that the solution to the stochastic differential equation (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) is given by</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=m_{t}X_{0}+m_{t}\int_{0}^{t}\sqrt{2\beta_{s}}/m_{s}dB_{s}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=m_{t}X_{0}+m_{t}\int_{0}^{t}\sqrt{2\beta_{s}}/m_{s}dB_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{t}=\exp(-\int_{0}^{t}\beta_{s}ds)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}=\exp(-\int_{0}^{t}\beta_{s}ds)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, the conditional distribution <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}|X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}|X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>) is a Gaussian <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(m_{t}X_{0},\sigma^{2}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(m_{t}X_{0},\sigma^{2}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> where
<math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}^{2}=1-m_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}^{2}=1-m_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus the conditional distribution <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}|X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}|X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has the density</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t|0}(x|X_{0}=x_{0})=\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{d}(2\pi)^{d/2}}\exp\left(-\frac{\left\lVert x-m_{t}x_{0}\right\rVert^{2}}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t|0}(x|X_{0}=x_{0})=\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}^{d}(2\pi)^{d/2}}\exp\left(-\frac{\left\lVert x-m_{t}x_{0}\right\rVert^{2}}{2\sigma_{t}^{2}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">We denote the marginal density <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)=p_{X_{t}}(x)=\int p_{t|0}(x|y)p_{0}(y)dy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)=p_{X_{t}}(x)=\int p_{t|0}(x|y)p_{0}(y)dy</annotation></semantics></math>, and the score function <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(x)=\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(x)=\nabla_{x}\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. We suppress the dependencies of <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for simplicity. The diffusion model first estimates the score function <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> by de-noising score matching, and then plugs the estimated score function <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> into the reverse SDE in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) to construct the estimator <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a realization from <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{p}</annotation></semantics></math> with an associated algorithm by discretizing (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this article, we analyze the performance of diffusion models with a standard configuration that performs the score matching using fully-connected deep ReLU neural networks. We show that the proposed estimator adapts to the low-dimensional structure of <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> in a structure-agnostic manner. We first introduce the neural network classes, score-matching estimator, and the constructed diffusion model in Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, and present the considered low-dimension structure in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Exponential-interaction model ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>The proposed diffusion model estimator</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fully connected deep ReLU neural networks.</span> We adopt the fully connected deep neural network with ReLU activation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)=\max\{\cdot,0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)=\max\{\cdot,0\}</annotation></semantics></math> to estimate the score function given its great empirical success. We refer to it as <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deep ReLU network</em> for short. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> be any positive integers. A deep ReLU network with depth <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> admits the form of</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle f(x)=\mathcal{T}_{L+1}\circ\bar{\sigma}\circ\mathcal{T}_{L}\circ\bar{\sigma}\circ\mathcal{T}_{L-1}\circ\bar{\sigma}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{T}_{2}\circ\bar{\sigma}\circ\mathcal{T}_{1}(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(x)=\mathcal{T}_{L+1}\circ\bar{\sigma}\circ\mathcal{T}_{L}\circ\bar{\sigma}\circ\mathcal{T}_{L-1}\circ\bar{\sigma}\circ\cdots\circ\mathcal{T}_{2}\circ\bar{\sigma}\circ\mathcal{T}_{1}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{i}(x)=A_{i}x+b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{i}(x)=A_{i}x+b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear transformation with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in\mathds{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{i-1}},b_{i}\in\mathds{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in\mathds{R}^{d_{i}\times d_{i-1}},b_{i}\in\mathds{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the natural number sequence <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(d_{0},d_{1},\cdots,d_{L},d_{L+1}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(d_{0},d_{1},\cdots,d_{L},d_{L+1}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> is specified as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,W,\cdots,W,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,W,\cdots,W,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\sigma}:\mathds{R}^{d_{i}}\rightarrow\mathds{R}^{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\sigma}:\mathds{R}^{d_{i}}\rightarrow\mathds{R}^{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> applies the ReLU activation function <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(x)=\max\{0,x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(x)=\max\{0,x\}</annotation></semantics></math> to each entry of a <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{d_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{d_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector. We define the family of <em id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deep ReLU networks taking <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vector as input with depth <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math></em> as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d,L,W)=\{g(x):g(x)~\text{in}~\eqref{equation:nn-def}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝖭𝖭</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>in</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>(</mtext><mtext><a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a></mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d,L,W)=\{g(x):g(x)~\text{in}~\eqref{equation:nn-def}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Score matching.</span>
It is required to first estimate the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">score function</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(x)=\nabla\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(x)=\nabla\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>
using observed data <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and then plug-in the estimated <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> into the backward process (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) for sampling. A standard method to estimate the score function is the <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">de-noising score-matching</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hyvärinen,, 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vincent,, 2011</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall the definition of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t|0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t|0}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vincent, (2011)</a></cite> establishes the following identity, which express the mean-square error for the score estimation as that for the conditional one. That is, for any <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:\mathds{R}^{d+1}\to\mathds{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:\mathds{R}^{d+1}\to\mathds{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\mathds{E}_{X_{t}}\left[\left\lVert s(X_{t},t)-\nabla\log p_{t}(X_{t})\right\rVert_{2}^{2}\right]\\
&amp;\qquad\qquad=\mathds{E}_{X_{t}\sim p_{t|0}(\cdot|X_{0}),X_{0}}\left[\left\lVert s(X_{t},t)-\nabla\log p_{t|0}(X_{t}\mid X_{0})\right\rVert_{2}^{2}\right]+C_{t,p_{0}},\end{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mo>‖</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>‖</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\mathds{E}_{X_{t}}\left[\left\lVert s(X_{t},t)-\nabla\log p_{t}(X_{t})\right\rVert_{2}^{2}\right]\\
&amp;\qquad\qquad=\mathds{E}_{X_{t}\sim p_{t|0}(\cdot|X_{0}),X_{0}}\left[\left\lVert s(X_{t},t)-\nabla\log p_{t|0}(X_{t}\mid X_{0})\right\rVert_{2}^{2}\right]+C_{t,p_{0}},\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t,p_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t,p_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant only relies on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> but independent of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. The beauty of this identity is that there is an explicit form for the conditional density function, and the constant is independent of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence the neural network weights in our approximation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\underline{T},\overline{T})\in\mathds{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\underline{T},\overline{T})\in\mathds{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be time hyper-parameters that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\underline{T}&lt;\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&lt;</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\underline{T}&lt;\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Motivated by (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>), for any <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:\mathds{R}^{d+1}\to\mathds{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:\mathds{R}^{d+1}\to\mathds{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the finite sample score matching loss over time interval <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\underline{T},\overline{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\underline{T},\overline{T}]</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n}(s)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{E}_{t\sim\mathsf{Unif}(\underline{T},\overline{T}),X_{t}\sim p_{t|0}(\cdot|X_{0,i})}\left[\lambda(t)\left\|s(X_{t},t)-\nabla\log p_{t|0}(X_{t}|X_{0,i})\right\|_{2}^{2}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n}(s)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{E}_{t\sim\mathsf{Unif}(\underline{T},\overline{T}),X_{t}\sim p_{t|0}(\cdot|X_{0,i})}\left[\lambda(t)\left\|s(X_{t},t)-\nabla\log p_{t|0}(X_{t}|X_{0,i})\right\|_{2}^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{i,0}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{i,0}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are given data, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Unif}(\underline{T},\overline{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Unif}(\underline{T},\overline{T})</annotation></semantics></math> is the uniform distribution on the time interval <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\underline{T},\overline{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\underline{T},\overline{T}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t|0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t|0}</annotation></semantics></math> has a parametric form in (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) such that one can easily sample <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\nabla_{x}\log p_{t|0}(x|z)=-(x-m_{t}z)/\sigma_{t}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{x}\log p_{t|0}(x|z)=-(x-m_{t}z)/\sigma_{t}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we choose <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(t)\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(t)\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Note that the loss function in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>) involves continuous time sampling and expectation over the normal distribution <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t|0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t|0}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, the expectation over <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\sim\mathsf{Unif}(\underline{T},\overline{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\sim\mathsf{Unif}(\underline{T},\overline{T})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{t}\sim p_{t}|0(\cdot|X_{0,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\sim p_{t}|0(\cdot|X_{0,i})</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by Monte Carlo sampling and further be optimized using gradient descent algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kloeden et al.,, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon,, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Bortoli,, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Benton et al.,, 2023</a>)</cite>. In this paper, we focus on the semi-empirical loss (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>) and solve the continuous-time SDE in the next step for simplicity of presentation. We emphasize that our main result can be easily extended to the fully empirical level loss with a proper time discretization by adopting a similar idea in Section 5.3 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The neural network score-matching estimator is</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{s}:=\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W,B)}\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n}(s)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝖭𝖭</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{s}:=\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W,B)}\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n}(s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where the truncated neural network class is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W,B)=\left\{\tau\Big(f(x,t);B\sigma_{t}^{-1}\sqrt{\log(WL)}\Big),f(x,t)\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W)\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝖭𝖭</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝖭𝖭</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W,B)=\left\{\tau\Big(f(x,t);B\sigma_{t}^{-1}\sqrt{\log(WL)}\Big),f(x,t)\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W)\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(z;\rho)=\mathrm{sign}(z)\min\{|z|,\rho\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(z;\rho)=\mathrm{sign}(z)\min\{|z|,\rho\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the truncation hyper-parameter to be determined.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion model with de-noising score-matching.</span>
We propose the distribution estimator <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by solving the backward process
with the estimated score function <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>). More precisely, we regard the density of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioned on the data, as an implicit density estimator of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, equation (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In 5 ‣ Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>) also provides a method to sample the data <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\overline{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\overline{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) converges to the standard Gaussian in an exponential rate as <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}\rightarrow+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}\rightarrow+\infty</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bakry et al.,, 2014</a>)</cite>, we pick a large enough <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}\asymp\log(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≍</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}\asymp\log(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and replace <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\overline{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\overline{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) by standard normal distribution. The process is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Note that <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is a transform of the noise <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Y}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Y}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and Brownian motion <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{B_{t}\}_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{B_{t}\}_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which serves as an implicit estimator for the target distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Implicit distribution estimator based on diffusion models</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            i.i.d. samples <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from the distribution <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            an output <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn for an implicitly estimated density that is close to <math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> for large <math id="alg1.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            Set the network parameters (depth <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, width <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, and truncation threshold <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>)
and time parameters <math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\underline{T},\overline{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\underline{T},\overline{T})</annotation></semantics></math> as specified in Condition <a href="#S3.Thmcondition3" title="Condition 3.3 (Choice of parameters). ‣ 3.2 Convergence rate in TV distance ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            Compute the estimated score function
<math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> by solving <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}:=\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W,B)}\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝖭𝖭</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}:=\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W,B)}\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the loss <math id="alg1.l4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
            Draw the sample from the backward process with the estimated score <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math>:

<table id="S2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d\widehat{Y}_{t}=\beta_{\overline{T}-t}\left(\widehat{Y}_{t}+2\hat{s}(\widehat{Y}_{t},\overline{T}-t)\right)dt+\sqrt{2\beta_{\overline{T}-t}}dB_{t},\,\widehat{Y}_{0}\sim N(0,I_{d})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\widehat{Y}_{t}=\beta_{\overline{T}-t}\left(\widehat{Y}_{t}+2\hat{s}(\widehat{Y}_{t},\overline{T}-t)\right)dt+\sqrt{2\beta_{\overline{T}-t}}dB_{t},\,\widehat{Y}_{0}\sim N(0,I_{d}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Y}_{\overline{T}-\underline{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Y}_{\overline{T}-\underline{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Exponential-interaction model</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first introduce the <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth function class.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=q+s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=q+s</annotation></semantics></math> for some natural number <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;s\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;s\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-variate function <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathds{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathds{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathds{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathds{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth if for every sequence <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\alpha_{j}\right\}_{j=1}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\alpha_{j}\right\}_{j=1}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> of
natural numbers such that <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j=1}^{d}\alpha_{j}=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j=1}^{d}\alpha_{j}=q</annotation></semantics></math>, the partial derivative <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\partial f)/\left(\partial x_{1}^{\alpha_{1}}\cdots\partial^{q}x_{d}^{\alpha_{d}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>q</mi></msup><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\partial f)/\left(\partial x_{1}^{\alpha_{1}}\cdots\partial^{q}x_{d}^{\alpha_{d}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> exists and satisfies for any <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathds{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathds{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> that</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\frac{\partial^{q}f}{\partial x_{1}^{\alpha_{1}}\cdots\partial x_{d}^{\alpha_{d}}}(x)-\frac{\partial^{q}f}{\partial y_{1}^{\alpha_{1}}\cdots\partial y_{d}^{\alpha_{d}}}(y)\right|\leq C\|x-y\|_{2}^{s}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>q</mi></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∂</mo><mi>q</mi></msup><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{\partial^{q}f}{\partial x_{1}^{\alpha_{1}}\cdots\partial x_{d}^{\alpha_{d}}}(x)-\frac{\partial^{q}f}{\partial y_{1}^{\alpha_{1}}\cdots\partial y_{d}^{\alpha_{d}}}(y)\right|\leq C\|x-y\|_{2}^{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is known that estimating the density for the <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-variate <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-function class
from <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. observations has the minimax estimation error as <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-2\beta/(2\beta+d)}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khas’minskii,, 1979</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stone,, 1982</a>)</cite>. The rates are extremely slow when the input dimension <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is very large, with the effect degree of smooth <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta/d</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>, which is common in neural network applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poggio et al.,, 2017</a>)</cite>. In this paper, we consider the following low-dimensional structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Exponential-interaction model)<span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a nature number <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}\leq d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}\leq d</annotation></semantics></math> and real numbers <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta,C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta,C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the exponential-interaction function class
<math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)=\left\{p(x)=\prod_{J\subseteq[d],|J|\leq d^{*}}f_{J}(x_{J}):~f_{J}\text{ is }(\beta,C)\text{-smooth}\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.608em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> is </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-smooth</mtext></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)=\left\{p(x)=\prod_{J\subseteq[d],|J|\leq d^{*}}f_{J}(x_{J}):~f_{J}\text{ is }(\beta,C)\text{-smooth}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A similar model class (additive rather than productive) has been studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattacharya et al., (2024)</a></cite> for understanding the performance of high-dimensional neural network regression. We provide several examples to show the versatility of our model class.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmremark2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(a)</span><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
Our model class </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> captures any </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-smooth density </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\mathds{R}^{d^{*}}\rightarrow\mathds{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\mathds{R}^{d^{*}}\rightarrow\mathds{R}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
For any </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J\subset[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J\subset[d]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with size </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*},</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> we set
</span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{J}=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{J}=p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=[d^{*}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=[d^{*}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> else wise.
In this way, Definition </span><a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Exponential-interaction model). ‣ 2.2 Exponential-interaction model ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> recovers the density </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmremark2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(b)</span><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
By the Hammersley-Clifford theorem </span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(</span><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wainwright,, 2019</a><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">)</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, our distribution class
captures any distribution satisfying the local Markov property with respect to a graph with </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> vertices whose maximum clique size is </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. For instance, the Markov random field in the left panel of Figure </span><a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Remark 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Exponential-interaction model ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> belongs to our density class with </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, which has the density </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)\propto f_{12}(x_{1},x_{2})f_{23}(x_{2},x_{3})f_{34}(x_{3},x_{4})f_{14}(x_{1},x_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>12</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>23</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>34</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>14</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)\propto f_{12}(x_{1},x_{2})f_{23}(x_{2},x_{3})f_{34}(x_{3},x_{4})f_{14}(x_{1},x_{4})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmremark2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(c)</span><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Our distribution class also covers Bayesian networks.
For instance, the Bayesian network in the right panel of Figure </span><a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Remark 2.2. ‣ 2.2 Exponential-interaction model ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
belongs to our density class with </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, which has the density
</span><math id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)\propto p(x_{1})p(x_{2}\mid x_{1})p(x_{3}\mid x_{2})p(x_{4}\mid x_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)\propto p(x_{1})p(x_{2}\mid x_{1})p(x_{3}\mid x_{2})p(x_{4}\mid x_{3})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmremark2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03994v1/MRF.svg" id="S2.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:119/114;" width="119" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:80%;">Markov random field</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S2.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03994v1/BN.svg" id="S2.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:119/114;" width="119" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.sf2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S2.F1.sf2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright" style="font-size:80%;">Bayesian network</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Figure 1</span>: </span>The illustrating examples with 4 variables (<math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=4</annotation></semantics></math>) and <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. For the Markov random field, the edge between node <math id="S2.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>
indicates that variables <math id="S2.F1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> may not be independent given all the other variables.
For the Bayesian network, the arrow from node <math id="S2.F1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.F1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>
indicates that <math id="S2.F1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct cause of <math id="S2.F1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Main result</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first impose some regularity conditions widely adopted in the density estimation literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmcondition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_condition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmcondition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Condition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmcondition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower and upper bounded)<span id="S3.Thmcondition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmcondition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmcondition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmcondition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The density <math id="S3.Thmcondition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is supported on the unite cube <math id="S3.Thmcondition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. There exists a constant <math id="S3.Thmcondition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmcondition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/c_{1}\leq p_{0}(x)\leq c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/c_{1}\leq p_{0}(x)\leq c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.Thmcondition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[-1,1]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[-1,1]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the diffusion model estimator is based on the weighting function <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> since the target score function <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}=\nabla\log p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}=\nabla\log p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and the reverse backward process (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1.1 Diffusion models and the problem under study ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>) depend explicitly on <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Condition <a href="#S3.Thmcondition2" title="Condition 3.2. ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> below shows that our result applies to various choices of <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying some weak regularity conditions. This includes some widely adopted choices of <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, including, for example, the constant function in the Langevin dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song and Ermon,, 2019</a>)</cite> and the linear function in the DDPM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al.,, 2020</a>)</cite>, in previous literature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmcondition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_condition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmcondition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Condition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmcondition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmcondition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmcondition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmcondition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a constant <math id="S3.Thmcondition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="S3.Thmcondition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in\mathds{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in\mathds{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmcondition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the derivatives <math id="S3.Thmcondition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\frac{d^{\ell}}{dt}\beta_{t}\right|\leq c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\frac{d^{\ell}}{dt}\beta_{t}\right|\leq c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmcondition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/c_{2}\leq\beta_{t}\leq c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/c_{2}\leq\beta_{t}\leq c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Score approximation error</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(x)=\nabla\log p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(x)=\nabla\log p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the score function of the diffused density <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The following theorem establishes the approximation error of fully connected neural networks with respect to the function <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{*}(t,x)=s_{t}(x):\mathds{R}^{d+1}\to\mathds{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{*}(t,x)=s_{t}(x):\mathds{R}^{d+1}\to\mathds{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>. The technical proof is highly complex and technical, with over 100 pages. We therefore relegate all the proofs to the appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Approximation error)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume Conditions <a href="#S3.Thmcondition1" title="Condition 3.1 (Lower and upper bounded). ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmcondition2" title="Condition 3.2. ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> hold.
There exists constants <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{3},c_{4},c_{5},c_{6},c_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{3},c_{4},c_{5},c_{6},c_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> depending only on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{1},c_{2},\beta,d,d^{*},C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{1},c_{2},\beta,d,d^{*},C)</annotation></semantics></math> such that the following holds.
For any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W,L</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min\{W,L\}\geq\left(1+\log(WL)\right)^{c_{5}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min\{W,L\}\geq\left(1+\log(WL)\right)^{c_{5}}</annotation></semantics></math>, letting <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{T}=(WL)^{-c_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{T}=(WL)^{-c_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}=c_{4}\log(WL)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}=c_{4}\log(WL)</annotation></semantics></math>, and any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>,
there exists a neural network <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{s}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W,c_{6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝖭𝖭</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{s}\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L,W,c_{6})</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left[\int_{x\in\mathds{R}^{d}}\left\lVert\tilde{s}(x,t)-s_{t}(x)\right\rVert^{2}p_{t}(x)dx\right]^{1/2}\leq c_{5}\frac{(WL)^{-2\beta/d^{*}}}{\sigma_{t}}\big(\log(WL)\big)^{c_{7}}\qquad\forall t\in[\underline{T},\overline{T}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><msub><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left[\int_{x\in\mathds{R}^{d}}\left\lVert\tilde{s}(x,t)-s_{t}(x)\right\rVert^{2}p_{t}(x)dx\right]^{1/2}\leq c_{5}\frac{(WL)^{-2\beta/d^{*}}}{\sigma_{t}}\big(\log(WL)\big)^{c_{7}}\qquad\forall t\in[\underline{T},\overline{T}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The dependency of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{3},\ldots,c_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{3},\ldots,c_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{1},c_{2},\beta,d,d^{*},C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{1},c_{2},\beta,d,d^{*},C)</annotation></semantics></math> can be found in
Condition D.1.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Approximation error). ‣ 3.1 Score approximation error ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is the first neural network score function approximation result that can further yield the optimal estimation error rate when <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a low-dimensional interaction model structure. It indicates that the approximate error depends on the effective dimension-adjusted degree of smoothness <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta/d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta/d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. There is a considerable literature on establishing deep ReLU networks’ approximation ability on the diffused score function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to specific function classes. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite> shows if <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by a sequence of fixed basis functions at the rate <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{-\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{-\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, namely,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall N,\qquad\inf_{a_{1},\ldots,a_{k}}\left\|\sum_{k=1}^{N}a_{k}\phi_{k}(x)-p_{0}(x)\right\|_{\infty}\lesssim N^{-\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.889em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall N,\qquad\inf_{a_{1},\ldots,a_{k}}\left\|\sum_{k=1}^{N}a_{k}\phi_{k}(x)-p_{0}(x)\right\|_{\infty}\lesssim N^{-\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">for a sequence of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> functions <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{l}(x)\}_{l=1}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{l}(x)\}_{l=1}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math>, then there exists a neural network with number of parameters <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> that can approximate <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> at the error rate <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}}S^{-\gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\sigma_{t}}S^{-\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly for all the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on this, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite> establishes an approximation result that can further yield minimax optimal estimation error for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth function class by adopting B-spline basis functions giving <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\beta/d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\beta/d</annotation></semantics></math>. This idea can be further extended to the Besov function class. However, this will suffer from the curse of dimensionality, resulting in a slow rate when ambient dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is relatively large compared with the smooth index <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, and the proof strategy cannot yield an optimal error bound for the class <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math> we study here.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">There are also some preliminary attempts showing that neural networks can be adaptive to low-dimensional structures like <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>, for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cole and Lu, (2024)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., (2025)</a></cite>. However, the analysis of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cole and Lu, (2024)</a></cite> depends on the additional fundamental assumption that all the derivatives of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are upper bounded by a constant <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&lt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&lt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, and the obtained rate is not optimal. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., (2025)</a></cite> achieve a similar result to our Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Approximation error). ‣ 3.1 Score approximation error ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> and further Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Error bound in TV distance). ‣ 3.2 Convergence rate in TV distance ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> when <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>, but they adopt their own designed weight-sharing network that essentially performs convolution efficiently, and their results are inapplicable to fully-connected or even standard convolutional neural networks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the class <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>, it is easy to see that the diffused function <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> admits approximately low-dimensional interaction structure when <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is small enough, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma_{t}\lesssim(WL)^{-\beta/d^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≲</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\lesssim(WL)^{-\beta/d^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is super-smooth akin to a <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> function when <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is large, i.e., <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma_{t}\gtrsim(WL)^{-1/d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≳</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\gtrsim(WL)^{-1/d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The main difficulty here is to establish the same fast rate in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In Theorem 3.1 (Approximation error). ‣ 3.1 Score approximation error ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) when <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(WL)^{-\beta/d^{*}}\ll\sigma_{t}\ll(WL)^{-1/d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(WL)^{-\beta/d^{*}}\ll\sigma_{t}\ll(WL)^{-1/d}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the regime where the diffused noise <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is not negligible such that the low-dimensional factorization structure no longer exists, while <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is also not large enough such that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is not super-smooth. Our main argument is that <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as a composition of either low-dimensional or super smooth functions under the general regime <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[\underline{T},\overline{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[\underline{T},\overline{T}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We offer a sketch of the proof for Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Approximation error). ‣ 3.1 Score approximation error ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">One improvement compared with previous work like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., (2025)</a></cite> is that we no longer need a technical assumption stating that <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is super smooth near the boundary of the support, i.e., Assumption 2.6 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite> and Assumption B in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., (2025)</a></cite>. In their proofs, such a technical assumption is imposed to establish approximation error for the score function <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\log p_{t}(x)=\frac{\nabla p_{t}(x)}{p_{t}(x)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\log p_{t}(x)=\frac{\nabla p_{t}(x)}{p_{t}(x)}</annotation></semantics></math> outside the region <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in[-m_{t},m_{t}]^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in[-m_{t},m_{t}]^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is small thus the approximation error of <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be upper bounded at the optimal rate without this technical assumption. In our proof, we introduce a super-smooth re-weighting function <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x)/\Phi(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x)/\Phi(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is lower bounded from below. Given super-smooth nature of <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>, we can approximate <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi/p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi/p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(\Phi\sigma_{t})^{-1}\nabla p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\Phi\sigma_{t})^{-1}\nabla p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> both at the rate of <math id="S3.Thmremark1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\tilde{O}((WL)^{-2\beta/d^{*}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{O}((WL)^{-2\beta/d^{*}})</annotation></semantics></math>, this allows us to get rid of such a technical assumption.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Compared with previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al.,, 2025</a>)</cite>, we achieve optimal approximation error for the diffused score function using deep neural networks with arbitrary (large enough) depth and width, and more importantly, do not require the use of neural networks with sparse weights. This is more practical, and requires much more effort in theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al.,, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohler and Langer,, 2021</a>)</cite>. On the contrary, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., (2025)</a></cite> use a parallelization of <math id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> sub-networks with width <math id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> for approximating score functions when <math id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="t\gtrsim(WL)^{-4/d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≳</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\gtrsim(WL)^{-4/d}</annotation></semantics></math>, but this inevitably place sparse constraint on neural networks classes (width <math id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmremark2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(W)</annotation></semantics></math> non-zero parameters).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Though our approximation result is stated specifically for
densities of the functional form
<math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=\exp(\sum_{|J|\leq d^{*}}f_{J}(x_{J}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=\exp(\sum_{|J|\leq d^{*}}f_{J}(x_{J}))</annotation></semantics></math>, our main arguments and the results also holds when <math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}=g(\sum_{|J|\leq d^{*}}f_{J}(x_{J}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}=g(\sum_{|J|\leq d^{*}}f_{J}(x_{J}))</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> function <math id="S3.Thmremark3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Convergence rate in TV distance</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the help of the above approximation error result, we are ready to state the convergence rate in TV distance between the distributions of the generated samples <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ground-truth <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a low-dimensional interaction structure. The following Condition <a href="#S3.Thmcondition3" title="Condition 3.3 (Choice of parameters). ‣ 3.2 Convergence rate in TV distance ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> specifies the hyperparameters to attain the optimal rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmcondition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_condition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmcondition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Condition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmcondition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Choice of parameters)<span id="S3.Thmcondition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W,L</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="WL=n^{\frac{d^{*}}{2(2\beta+d^{*})}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mfrac><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">WL=n^{\frac{d^{*}}{2(2\beta+d^{*})}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{W,L\}\geq\left(1+\log(WL)\right)^{c_{5}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{W,L\}\geq\left(1+\log(WL)\right)^{c_{5}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we pick other hyper-parameters as <math id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{T}=(WL)^{-c_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{T}=(WL)^{-c_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}=c_{4}\log(WL)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}=c_{4}\log(WL)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=c_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=c_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmcondition3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{3},\ldots,c_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{3},\ldots,c_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> are given in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Approximation error). ‣ 3.1 Score approximation error ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Error bound in TV distance)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume Conditions <a href="#S3.Thmcondition1" title="Condition 3.1 (Lower and upper bounded). ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmcondition2" title="Condition 3.2. ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, and <a href="#S3.Thmcondition3" title="Condition 3.3 (Choice of parameters). ‣ 3.2 Convergence rate in TV distance ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> hold.
There exists constants <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{8},c_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{8},c_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> depending only on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{1},c_{2},\beta,d,d^{*},C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{1},c_{2},\beta,d,d^{*},C)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq c_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq c_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathds{E}_{\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}}\left[\mathsf{TV}(p_{0},p_{\widehat{X}})\right]\leq c_{8}{n^{-\frac{\beta}{2\beta+d^{*}}}}(\log n)^{c_{9}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathds{E}_{\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}}\left[\mathsf{TV}(p_{0},p_{\widehat{X}})\right]\leq c_{8}{n^{-\frac{\beta}{2\beta+d^{*}}}}(\log n)^{c_{9}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\widehat{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\widehat{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution of the samples generated in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.1 The proposed diffusion model estimator ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math> contains at least all the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-variate <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth functions. Thus, the lower bound of the minimax optimal estimation error over <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-variate <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth functions (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khas’minskii, (1979)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stone, (1982)</a></cite>) gives</p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\inf_{\hat{p}}\sup_{p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)}\mathds{E}_{\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\sim p_{0}}\left[\mathsf{TV}(p_{0},\hat{p})\right]\gtrsim n^{-\frac{\beta}{2\beta+d^{*}}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>𝖳𝖵</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≳</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf_{\hat{p}}\sup_{p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)}\mathds{E}_{\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\sim p_{0}}\left[\mathsf{TV}(p_{0},\hat{p})\right]\gtrsim n^{-\frac{\beta}{2\beta+d^{*}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which implies that the error rate in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Error bound in TV distance). ‣ 3.2 Convergence rate in TV distance ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> is minimax optimal (up to logarithmic factors). It is worth noticing that both the diffusion algorithm and the neural network architecture are blind to the interaction structure in Definition <a href="#S2.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2.2 (Exponential-interaction model). ‣ 2.2 Exponential-interaction model ‣ 2 Setup and methodology ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Therefore, our result indicates that the diffusion model can be adaptive to the low-dimensional interaction structure and is free of the curse-of-dimensionality <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n^{-\beta/(2\beta+d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-\beta/(2\beta+d)}</annotation></semantics></math> rate in a structure-agnostic manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">It is also worth mentioning that our stochastic analysis applies to neural networks with unbounded weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Farrell et al.,, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kohler and Langer,, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. This is the regime where the previous proof strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al.,, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang and Yang,, 2024</a>)</cite> cannot apply because they rely on bounded weights constraints to establish covering number in <math id="S3.Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm; see technical novelties and discussions in Appendix D.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the key idea of our proof, here we provide a proof sketch for approximating the diffused density <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. The approximation for the corresponding score function <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math> is similar. Additional techniques are required when <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, which will be sketched at the end of this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here to simplify the presentation, we consider using neural networks with <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> depth and width <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> to achieve the approximation error <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N^{-2\beta/d^{*}})=\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N^{-2\beta/d^{*}})=\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. We also assume <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=O(1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Letting <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be the width of the neural network considered, we consider the approximation of <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the region <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Omega=\{(t,x):t\in[N^{-10},N^{-1/(10d)}],x\in[-m_{t},m_{t}]^{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=\{(t,x):t\in[N^{-10},N^{-1/(10d)}],x\in[-m_{t},m_{t}]^{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The approximation of <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> outside the region is potentially easier. Under this time horizon of <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, one has <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}\in[1/2,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}\in[1/2,2]</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is large, both <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> functions by Condition <a href="#S3.Thmcondition2" title="Condition 3.2. ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Denote <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}:=\{(i,j):i,j\in[d]\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}:=\{(i,j):i,j\in[d]\}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1</annotation></semantics></math>, one can write <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}(x)=\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}}f_{i,j}(x_{i},x_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}(x)=\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}}f_{i,j}(x_{i},x_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{i,j}(x_{i},x_{j})\}_{i,j\in[d]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{i,j}(x_{i},x_{j})\}_{i,j\in[d]}</annotation></semantics></math> are all Lipschitz functions. Our analysis is based on a novel decomposition of <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that factorizes <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as a sum of terms, where each term is a product of either low-dimension or super-smooth functions. For any <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in[d]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in[d]</annotation></semantics></math>, define the the residual <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i,j}:\mathds{R}^{5}\rightarrow\mathds{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i,j}:\mathds{R}^{5}\rightarrow\mathds{R}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j}):=f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}+\sigma_{t}y_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}+\sigma_{t}y_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)-f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j}):=f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}+\sigma_{t}y_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}+\sigma_{t}y_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)-f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}}{m_{t}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="K(y)=(2\pi)^{-d/2}\exp\left(-\|y\|^{2}/2\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(y)=(2\pi)^{-d/2}\exp\left(-\|y\|^{2}/2\right)</annotation></semantics></math> be the Gaussian density. Then, it follows from the definition of the <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the change of variable formula that</p>
<table id="S4.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(m_{t})^{-d}\int\overbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}}f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}+\sigma_{t}y_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}+\sigma_{t}y_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)}^{p_{0}((x+\sigma_{t}y)/m_{t})}K(y)dy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mover><mover accent="true"><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏞</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(m_{t})^{-d}\int\overbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}}f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}+\sigma_{t}y_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}+\sigma_{t}y_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)}^{p_{0}((x+\sigma_{t}y)/m_{t})}K(y)dy</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{(a)}{=}(m_{t})^{-d}\sum_{\mathcal{A}\subset\mathcal{S}}\underbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}\backslash\mathcal{A}}f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)}_{G_{\mathcal{S}\backslash\mathcal{A}}(x,t)}\underbrace{\int\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}}\Delta_{i,j}K(y)dy}_{\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{(a)}{=}(m_{t})^{-d}\sum_{\mathcal{A}\subset\mathcal{S}}\underbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}\backslash\mathcal{A}}f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)}_{G_{\mathcal{S}\backslash\mathcal{A}}(x,t)}\underbrace{\int\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}}\Delta_{i,j}K(y)dy}_{\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where in <math id="S4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)</annotation></semantics></math> we apply <math id="S4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}}(a_{i,j}+b_{i,j})=\sum_{\mathcal{A}\subset\mathcal{S}}\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}}a_{i,j}\prod_{(i,j)\notin\mathcal{A}}b_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}}(a_{i,j}+b_{i,j})=\sum_{\mathcal{A}\subset\mathcal{S}}\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}}a_{i,j}\prod_{(i,j)\notin\mathcal{A}}b_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i,j}=\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i,j}=\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i,j}=f_{i,j}(x_{i}/m_{t},y_{i}/m_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i,j}=f_{i,j}(x_{i}/m_{t},y_{i}/m_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The approximation of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is relatively simple: it is a finite composition of several bivariate Lipschitz functions <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{i,j}\}_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{i,j}\}_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and univariate <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> function <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the product function <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\rightarrow xy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\rightarrow xy</annotation></semantics></math> and the reciprocal function <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\rightarrow 1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\rightarrow 1/x</annotation></semantics></math> in the region <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1/2,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1/2,2]</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{t}\in[1/2,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{t}\in[1/2,2]</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, it follows from the approximation result for bivariate Lipschitz functions (see Theorem 1.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., (2021)</a></cite>) and the composition nature of neural networks that there exists a neural network <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with depth <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N\log N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N\log N)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S4.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\forall(t,x)\in\Omega,\qquad|\widetilde{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(t,x)-{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(t,x)|\lesssim N^{-2\beta/d^{*}}=N^{-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≲</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall(t,x)\in\Omega,\qquad|\widetilde{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(t,x)-{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(t,x)|\lesssim N^{-2\beta/d^{*}}=N^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The approximation for <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is much more involved. For given <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the time step satisfying <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t_{*}}=N^{-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}|}}(\log N)^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t_{*}}=N^{-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}|}}(\log N)^{7}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given the monotonicity of <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, one has <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\leq\sigma_{t_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\leq\sigma_{t_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq t_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq t_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\geq\sigma_{t_{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\geq\sigma_{t_{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq t_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq t_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our main argument is that <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is small enough when <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq t_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq t_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it can be decomposed into a composition of either super-smooth or low-dimensional functions when <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq t_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq t_{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, one has <math id="S4.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})|\lesssim\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})|\lesssim\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S4.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is Lipschitz function and <math id="S4.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/m_{t}\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/m_{t}\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, this means</p>
<table id="S4.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall t\in[N^{-10},t_{*}],\qquad|\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|\lesssim(\sigma_{t})^{|\mathcal{A}|}\leq N^{-1}(\log N)^{10d^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall t\in[N^{-10},t_{*}],\qquad|\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|\lesssim(\sigma_{t})^{|\mathcal{A}|}\leq N^{-1}(\log N)^{10d^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, similar to the decomposition in (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), one also has</p>
<table id="S4.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)=\sum_{\mathcal{B}\subset\mathcal{A}}(-1)^{\left|\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{B}\right|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)=\sum_{\mathcal{B}\subset\mathcal{A}}(-1)^{\left|\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{B}\right|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{B}}f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)}_{G_{\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B}}(x,t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{B}}f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)}_{G_{\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B}}(x,t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\times\underbrace{\int\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{B}}f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}+\sigma_{t}y_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}+\sigma_{t}y_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)K(y)dy}_{p_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\times\underbrace{\int\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{B}}f_{i,j}\left(\frac{x_{i}+\sigma_{t}y_{i}}{m_{t}},\frac{x_{j}+\sigma_{t}y_{j}}{m_{t}}\right)K(y)dy}_{p_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for any fixed <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t,\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t,\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a function dependent on at most <math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2|\mathcal{B}|\leq 2|\mathcal{A}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2|\mathcal{B}|\leq 2|\mathcal{A}|</annotation></semantics></math> variables. Moreover, our
Lemma E.2 claims that all the <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-th order derivatives of <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t,\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t,\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> are upper bounded by <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}(c_{\texttt{dr},1}\ell^{4})^{\ell}\sigma_{t}^{-\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dr</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}(c_{\texttt{dr},1}\ell^{4})^{\ell}\sigma_{t}^{-\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> for a constant <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\texttt{dr},1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dr</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\texttt{dr},1}</annotation></semantics></math> only dependent on <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{1},c_{2},d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{1},c_{2},d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>. Now we apply
Theorem A.18, a finer argument of Theorem 1.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., (2021)</a></cite> that is uniformly in time <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, there exists a neural network <math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with depth <math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log^{16}N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>16</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log^{16}N)</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N)</annotation></semantics></math> such that, <math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall(t,x)\in\Omega~\text{with}~t\geq t_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>with</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall(t,x)\in\Omega~\text{with}~t\geq t_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> we have for
<math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=\log(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=\log(N)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|\tilde{p}_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x,t)-p_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x,t)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|\tilde{p}_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x,t)-p_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x,t)|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq{\ell}^{d}\left\lVert\nabla^{\ell}p_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}N^{-\frac{\ell}{\left|\mathcal{B}\right|}}\leq c_{1}\ell^{d}\left(\frac{c_{\texttt{dr},1}\ell^{4}}{\sigma_{t}N^{\frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{B}\right|}}}\right)^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dr</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq{\ell}^{d}\left\lVert\nabla^{\ell}p_{t}\right\rVert_{\infty}N^{-\frac{\ell}{\left|\mathcal{B}\right|}}\leq c_{1}\ell^{d}\left(\frac{c_{\texttt{dr},1}\ell^{4}}{\sigma_{t}N^{\frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{B}\right|}}}\right)^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overset{(a)}{\leq}c_{1}(\log N)^{d}\left(\frac{\log N}{c_{\texttt{dr},1}}\right)^{-\log N}=O(N^{-10})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">a</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">dr</mtext><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overset{(a)}{\leq}c_{1}(\log N)^{d}\left(\frac{\log N}{c_{\texttt{dr},1}}\right)^{-\log N}=O(N^{-10}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a)</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the fact that <math id="S4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left|\mathcal{B}\right|\leq\left|\mathcal{A}\right|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left|\mathcal{B}\right|\leq\left|\mathcal{A}\right|</annotation></semantics></math> and the time step is large <math id="S4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq t_{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq t_{*}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}N^{1/|\mathcal{A}|}\geq\log^{5}(N)\geq\log(N)\ell^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}N^{1/|\mathcal{A}|}\geq\log^{5}(N)\geq\log(N)\ell^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, for any <math id="S4.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can use a neural network <math id="S4.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with depth <math id="S4.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\log^{2}N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\log^{2}N)</annotation></semantics></math> width <math id="S4.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N\log^{2d}N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>log</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N\log^{2d}N)</annotation></semantics></math> to approximate <math id="S4.p6.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="1\{t\geq t_{*}\}\times\sum_{\mathcal{B}\subset\mathcal{A}}\widetilde{G}_{\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B}}(x,t)\cdot\tilde{p}_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">×</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\{t\geq t_{*}\}\times\sum_{\mathcal{B}\subset\mathcal{A}}\widetilde{G}_{\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B}}(x,t)\cdot\tilde{p}_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with error <math id="S4.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{-10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{-10})</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining it with (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), <math id="S4.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S4.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall(t,x)\in\Omega,\qquad|\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)-\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|\lesssim N^{-1}(\log N)^{10d^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall(t,x)\in\Omega,\qquad|\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)-\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|\lesssim N^{-1}(\log N)^{10d^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>) together assert that both <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by neural network with depth <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> at the error rate <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Combing it with the decomposition (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) at the beginning, which states the target <math id="S4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of products of these terms <math id="S4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{G_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t),\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)\}_{\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{G_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t),\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)\}_{\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can find a neural network <math id="S4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}(t,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}(t,x)</annotation></semantics></math> with depth <math id="S4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> that can approximate <math id="S4.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly well at the error rate <math id="S4.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Extension to <math id="S4.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth noticing that the current decomposition (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) cannot yield optimal approximation error when <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. In this part, we illustrate the potential issue and a finer approximation when <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=2</annotation></semantics></math>; the extension to general <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is similar.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Turning to the decomposition (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), similar to the discussion above, for any <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with depth <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N\log N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N\log N)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S4.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\forall(t,x)\in\Omega,\qquad|\widetilde{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(t,x)-{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(t,x)|\lesssim N^{-2\beta/d^{*}}=N^{-2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>G</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≲</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall(t,x)\in\Omega,\qquad|\widetilde{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(t,x)-{G}_{\mathcal{S}\setminus\mathcal{A}}(t,x)|\lesssim N^{-2\beta/d^{*}}=N^{-2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, the arguments for <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> above cannot give the optimal approximation error for it. To be specific, following a similar discussion to the decomposition in (<a href="#S4.Ex3" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>), we can find a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> with depth <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math> and width <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S4.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\forall(t,x)\in\Omega\cap\{(t,x):t\geq t_{*,1}\},\qquad|\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)-\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|=\widetilde{O}(N^{-2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\forall(t,x)\in\Omega\cap\{(t,x):t\geq t_{*,1}\},\qquad|\widetilde{\Delta}_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)-\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|=\widetilde{O}(N^{-2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{*,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{*,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the timestep satisfying <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t_{*,1}}=N^{-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}|}}(\log N)^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t_{*,1}}=N^{-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}|}}(\log N)^{7}</annotation></semantics></math>. Here the choice of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{*,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{*,1}</annotation></semantics></math> is to let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}N^{1/|\mathcal{B}|}\geq\log^{7}(N)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}N^{1/|\mathcal{B}|}\geq\log^{7}(N)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq t_{*,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq t_{*,1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t,\mathcal{B}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}\subset\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}\subset\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>. The main issue here is that we no longer have <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|=\widetilde{O}(N^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|=\widetilde{O}(N^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq t_{*,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq t_{*,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is because akin to (<a href="#S4.E3" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>), one only has</p>
<table id="S4.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|\leq(\sigma_{t})^{|\mathcal{A}|(\beta\land 1)}=\sigma_{t}^{|\mathcal{A}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∧</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|\leq(\sigma_{t})^{|\mathcal{A}|(\beta\land 1)}=\sigma_{t}^{|\mathcal{A}|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">This implies that <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|=\widetilde{O}(N^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)|=\widetilde{O}(N^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math> only applies when <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}=\widetilde{O}(N^{-\frac{2}{|\mathcal{A}|}})=o(\sigma_{t_{*,1}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}=\widetilde{O}(N^{-\frac{2}{|\mathcal{A}|}})=o(\sigma_{t_{*,1}})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03994v1/decompo_betasmall.svg" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/132;" width="191" height="132"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Approximator for <math id="S4.F2.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03994v1/decompo_betalarge.svg" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/145;" width="191" height="145"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Approximator for <math id="S4.F2.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Figures illustrating the construction of the neural network approximator for <math id="S4.F2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The approximator for <math id="S4.F2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed in a bottom-to-top manner.
The blue arrow from “low rank” to node <math id="S4.F2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that <math id="S4.F2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a product of <math id="S4.F2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional Hölder-smooth functions, whose neural network approximators are constructed with
Theorem 1.1 in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., (2021)</a></cite>. For example, the nodes <math id="S4.F2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\mathcal{S}\backslash\mathcal{A}},G_{\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\mathcal{S}\backslash\mathcal{A}},G_{\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> are product of <math id="S4.F2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <math id="S4.F2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-functions, and
the node <math id="S4.F2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(1)}_{\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(1)}_{\mathcal{A}\backslash\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a product of <math id="S4.F2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <math id="S4.F2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta-1,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta-1,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-functions.
The red arrow from “smoothness” to node <math id="S4.F2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that <math id="S4.F2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is super-smooth or its parent node is negligible, whose neural network approximator is constructed with Theorem A.18.
For example, <math id="S4.F2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t,\mathcal{B}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t,\mathcal{B}}</annotation></semantics></math> is super-smooth otherwise <math id="S4.F2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is negligible.
Black arrows go from nodes <math id="S4.F2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.F2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> indicate that <math id="S4.F2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum-of-product of <math id="S4.F2.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y.</annotation></semantics></math> Thus the neural network approximator for <math id="S4.F2.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> can be constructed using the approximators for <math id="S4.F2.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F2.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y.</annotation></semantics></math>
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, a finer approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> in the time interval <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,t_{*,1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,t_{*,1}]</annotation></semantics></math>, especially when <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\gg N^{-\frac{2}{|\mathcal{A}|}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≫</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\gg N^{-\frac{2}{|\mathcal{A}|}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is required, this is also where high-order smoothness should come into play in the decomposition.
We first introduce some additional notations. Let <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}_{j}=x_{j}/m_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}_{j}=x_{j}/m_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\sigma}_{t}=\sigma_{t}/m_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\sigma}_{t}=\sigma_{t}/m_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We let
<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{i,j}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})=[\frac{\partial f_{i,j}}{\partial x_{i}}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j}),\frac{\partial f_{i,j}}{\partial x_{j}}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})]^{\top}\in\mathds{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{i,j}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})=[\frac{\partial f_{i,j}}{\partial x_{i}}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j}),\frac{\partial f_{i,j}}{\partial x_{j}}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})]^{\top}\in\mathds{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and define the scaled second order Taylor residual as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{i,j}^{(1)}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})=\frac{1}{\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}\left\{\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})-\tilde{\sigma}_{t}\cdot[y_{i},y_{j}]D_{i,j}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{i,j}^{(1)}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})=\frac{1}{\tilde{\sigma}_{t}}\left\{\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})-\tilde{\sigma}_{t}\cdot[y_{i},y_{j}]D_{i,j}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Plugging the identity <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})=\tilde{\sigma}_{t}\{\Delta_{i,j}^{(1)}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})+[y_{i},y_{j}]D_{i,j}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{i,j}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})=\tilde{\sigma}_{t}\{\Delta_{i,j}^{(1)}(t,x_{i},x_{j},y_{i},y_{j})+[y_{i},y_{j}]D_{i,j}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})\}</annotation></semantics></math> into the definition of <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>) then gives that</p>
<table id="S4.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\tilde{\sigma}_{t})^{|\mathcal{A}|}\sum_{\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{A}}\underbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{B}\setminus\mathcal{C}}D_{i,j,2}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})}_{G^{(1)}_{\mathcal{B}\setminus\mathcal{C},2}(x,t)}\times\underbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B}}D_{i,j,1}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})}_{G^{(1)}_{\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B},1}(x,t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\tilde{\sigma}_{t})^{|\mathcal{A}|}\sum_{\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{B}\subseteq\mathcal{A}}\underbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{B}\setminus\mathcal{C}}D_{i,j,2}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})}_{G^{(1)}_{\mathcal{B}\setminus\mathcal{C},2}(x,t)}\times\underbrace{\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B}}D_{i,j,1}(\tilde{x}_{i},\tilde{x}_{j})}_{G^{(1)}_{\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B},1}(x,t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\times\underbrace{\int\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{C}}\Delta_{i,j}^{(1)}\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B}}y_{i}\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{B}\setminus\mathcal{C}}y_{j}K(y)dy}_{\Delta^{(1)}_{\mathcal{C}}(x,t)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="9.462em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">d</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\times\underbrace{\int\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{C}}\Delta_{i,j}^{(1)}\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{A}\setminus\mathcal{B}}y_{i}\prod_{(i,j)\in\mathcal{B}\setminus\mathcal{C}}y_{j}K(y)dy}_{\Delta^{(1)}_{\mathcal{C}}(x,t)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">The idea is, the functions in <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{G_{\mathcal{K},l}^{(1)}(x,t)\}_{\mathcal{K}\subseteq\mathcal{S},l\in[2]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{G_{\mathcal{K},l}^{(1)}(x,t)\}_{\mathcal{K}\subseteq\mathcal{S},l\in[2]}</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by neural networks at the error rate <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N^{-(\beta-1)})=\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N^{-(\beta-1)})=\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> given each function itself is a composition of simple functions and <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-variate, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,C)</annotation></semantics></math> smooth functions. On the other hand, the same approximation error rate <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(N^{-(\beta-1)})=\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(N^{-(\beta-1)})=\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math> is also attainable for <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{C}}^{(1)}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{C}}^{(1)}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The idea is similar to <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> in the previous discussion: <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{C}}^{(1)}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{C}}^{(1)}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is negligible, i.e., <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\Delta_{\mathcal{C}}^{(1)}(x,t)|\leq\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\Delta_{\mathcal{C}}^{(1)}(x,t)|\leq\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, when <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\leq N^{-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}|}}(\log N)^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\leq N^{-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}|}}(\log N)^{7}</annotation></semantics></math>, and can be decomposition into a composition of either at least <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-variate <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth functions, or super-smooth functions similar to (<a href="#S4.Ex3" title="In 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>) as long as <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}\geq N^{-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}|}}(\log N)^{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}\geq N^{-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{C}|}}(\log N)^{7}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, when <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\leq t_{*,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\leq t_{*,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, one can construct a neural network that approximates <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathcal{A}}(x,t)</annotation></semantics></math> at the rate of</p>
<table id="S4.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{|\mathcal{A}|}\times\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})\lesssim(\sigma_{t_{*,1}})^{|\mathcal{A}|}\times\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})=\widetilde{O}(N^{-2})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≲</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{\sigma}_{t}^{|\mathcal{A}|}\times\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})\lesssim(\sigma_{t_{*,1}})^{|\mathcal{A}|}\times\widetilde{O}(N^{-1})=\widetilde{O}(N^{-2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The proof idea is summarized in the right panel of Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4.1 Extension to 𝛽&gt;1 ‣ 4 Proof sketch of the neural network approximation result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Convergence rates in <math id="S5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Besides the TV distance, we also measure the performance of the diffusion model distribution estimator in the Wasserstein distance of order one (<math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance).
For any distributions <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi(X,\widehat{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi(X,\widehat{X})</annotation></semantics></math> denote all joint distributions <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> that have marginals <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W_{1}(X,\widehat{X}):=\inf_{\pi\in\Pi(X,\widehat{X})}\int\|x-y\|d\pi(x,y)=\sup_{f:\mbox{ \scriptsize Lip}(f)\leq 1}|Ef(X)-f(\widehat{X})|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> Lip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}(X,\widehat{X}):=\inf_{\pi\in\Pi(X,\widehat{X})}\int\|x-y\|d\pi(x,y)=\sup_{f:\mbox{ \scriptsize Lip}(f)\leq 1}|Ef(X)-f(\widehat{X})|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Piecewise score estimator.</span>
To achieve an efficient estimator in <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance, we slightly modify the
score-matching procedure. We partition the time horizon <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\underline{T},\overline{T}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\underline{T},\overline{T}]</annotation></semantics></math> as consecutive intervals
<math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=\underline{T}&lt;t_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;t_{P}=\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=\underline{T}&lt;t_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;t_{P}=\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for time parameters specified later. The piecewise score estimator uses neural networks with varying sizes in time intervals <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{\ell-1},t_{\ell}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{\ell-1},t_{\ell}]</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in[P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in[P]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote the score-matching loss on the time interval <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{j-1},t_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{j-1},t_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n,j}(s)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{E}_{t\sim\mathsf{Unif}(t_{j-1},t_{j}),X_{t}\sim p_{t|0}(\cdot|X_{0,i})}\left[\left\|s(X_{t},t)-\nabla\log p_{t|0}(X_{t}|X_{0,i})\right\|_{2}^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n,j}(s)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathds{E}_{t\sim\mathsf{Unif}(t_{j-1},t_{j}),X_{t}\sim p_{t|0}(\cdot|X_{0,i})}\left[\left\|s(X_{t},t)-\nabla\log p_{t|0}(X_{t}|X_{0,i})\right\|_{2}^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Unif}([t_{j-1},t_{j}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Unif}([t_{j-1},t_{j}])</annotation></semantics></math> is the uniform distribution on the time interval <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{j-1},t_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{j-1},t_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
We then train the neural network score-matching estimator on <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{j-1},t_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{j-1},t_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{s}_{j}:=\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L_{j},W_{j},B_{j})}\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n,j}(s)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝖭𝖭</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{s}_{j}:=\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L_{j},W_{j},B_{j})}\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n,j}(s),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">for the network parameters <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L_{j},W_{j},B_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L_{j},W_{j},B_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> specified later.
The final score estimator <math id="S5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</p>
<table id="S5.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{s}(t,x)=\hat{s}_{j}(t,x)\text{ for any }t\in[t_{j-1},t_{j}],\,j\in[P]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for any </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{s}(t,x)=\hat{s}_{j}(t,x)\text{ for any }t\in[t_{j-1},t_{j}],\,j\in[P].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The underlying reason behind adopting the piecewise score estimator is that the score function estimation error in different timesteps contributed to the final <math id="S5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> error in a non-uniform manner. In particular, the score function estimation error for large timesteps contributes less to the final <math id="S5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> error, hence using ReLU neural network score function estimators with descending width/depth in timestep as in Condition <a href="#S5.Thmcondition1" title="Condition 5.1 (Choice of parameters). ‣ 5.1 Convergence rate in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> can attain a slightly faster rate; see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion model with piecewise score-matching.</span>
We propose the distribution estimator <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">W</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> by solving the backward process
with the estimated score function <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S5.E3" title="In 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>).
The process is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Diffusion models with piecewise score-matching.</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          i.i.d. samples <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> drawn from the distribution <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          an output <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">W</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose distribution is close to <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          Initialization: <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}\equiv 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}\equiv 0</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
            Set the network parameters (depth <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, width <math id="alg2.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and truncation threshold <math id="alg2.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) and the
time parameter <math id="alg2.l5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
as specified in Condition <a href="#S5.Thmcondition1" title="Condition 5.1 (Choice of parameters). ‣ 5.1 Convergence rate in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>.

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
            Compute the estimated score function <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="alg2.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{j-1},t_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{j-1},t_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math>
by minimizing the loss <math id="alg2.l6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n,j}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in (<a href="#S5.E1" title="In 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>) as
<math id="alg2.l6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}_{j}:=\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L_{j},W_{j},B_{j})}\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n,j}(s)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>𝖭𝖭</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖫</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}_{j}:=\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{F}_{\mathsf{NN}}(d+1,L_{j},W_{j},B_{j})}\widehat{\mathsf{L}}_{n,j}(s).</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
            Update the estimated score function
<math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{s}(t,x)\leftarrow\hat{s}(t,x)+\hat{s}_{j}(t,x)\mathds{1}\{t_{j-1}\leq t\leq t_{j}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}(t,x)\leftarrow\hat{s}(t,x)+\hat{s}_{j}(t,x)\mathds{1}\{t_{j-1}\leq t\leq t_{j}\}.</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
            
            
          Draw the sample from the backward process with the estimated score <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{s}</annotation></semantics></math>:

<table id="S5.EGx33" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d\widehat{Y}_{t}=\beta_{\overline{T}-t}\left(\widehat{Y}_{t}+2\hat{s}(\widehat{Y}_{t},\overline{T}-t)\right)dt+\sqrt{2\beta_{\overline{T}-t}}dB_{t},\,\widehat{Y}_{0}\sim N(0,I_{d})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\widehat{Y}_{t}=\beta_{\overline{T}-t}\left(\widehat{Y}_{t}+2\hat{s}(\widehat{Y}_{t},\overline{T}-t)\right)dt+\sqrt{2\beta_{\overline{T}-t}}dB_{t},\,\widehat{Y}_{0}\sim N(0,I_{d}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5.4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">W</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="alg2.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{Y}_{\overline{T}-\underline{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{Y}_{\overline{T}-\underline{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Convergence rate in <math id="S5.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We state the convergence rate in <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance between the generated samples <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">W</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the ground-truth <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p_{0}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p_{0}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a low-dimensional interaction structure. The following Condition <a href="#S5.Thmcondition1" title="Condition 5.1 (Choice of parameters). ‣ 5.1 Convergence rate in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> specifies the hyperparameters to attain the desired rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmcondition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_condition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmcondition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Condition 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmcondition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Choice of parameters)<span id="S5.Thmcondition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\lfloor\log_{2}(\overline{T}/\underline{T})\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>/</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\lfloor\log_{2}(\overline{T}/\underline{T})\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=\underline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=\underline{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{1}=n^{-\frac{2d^{*}}{d(2\beta+d^{*})}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{1}=n^{-\frac{2d^{*}}{d(2\beta+d^{*})}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{j+1}=2t_{j}\wedge\overline{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{j+1}=2t_{j}\wedge\overline{T}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[P-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[P-1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}L_{j}=t_{j}^{-\frac{d}{4}}(\log W_{1}L_{1})^{2c_{5}+500d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>500</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}L_{j}=t_{j}^{-\frac{d}{4}}(\log W_{1}L_{1})^{2c_{5}+500d}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{j}\wedge L_{j}\geq(1+\log(W_{1}L_{1}))^{c_{5}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{j}\wedge L_{j}\geq(1+\log(W_{1}L_{1}))^{c_{5}}</annotation></semantics></math>
for any <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\in[P]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\in[P]</annotation></semantics></math>, and we pick other hyper-parameters as <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{T}=(W_{1}L_{1})^{-c_{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{T}=(W_{1}L_{1})^{-c_{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{T}=c_{4}\log(W_{1}L_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{T}=c_{4}\log(W_{1}L_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{j}=c_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{j}=c_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.Thmcondition1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{3},\ldots,c_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{3},\ldots,c_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> are given in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1 (Approximation error). ‣ 3.1 Score approximation error ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Error bound in <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume Conditions <a href="#S3.Thmcondition1" title="Condition 3.1 (Lower and upper bounded). ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmcondition2" title="Condition 3.2. ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, and <a href="#S5.Thmcondition1" title="Condition 5.1 (Choice of parameters). ‣ 5.1 Convergence rate in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> hold.
There exists constants <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{8},c_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{8},c_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> depending only on <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{1},c_{2},\beta,d,d^{*},C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{1},c_{2},\beta,d,d^{*},C)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that for any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq c_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>8</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq c_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathds{E}_{\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}}\left[W_{1}(p_{0},p_{\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}})\right]\leq\begin{cases}c_{8}{n^{-\frac{\beta+\frac{d^{*}}{d}}{2\beta+d^{*}}}}(\log n)^{c_{9}},&amp;\text{ for }d\geq 2,\\
\\
c_{8}n^{-1/2}(\log n)^{c_{9}},&amp;\text{ for }d=1.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">W</mtext></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>8</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>9</mn></msub></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathds{E}_{\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}}\left[W_{1}(p_{0},p_{\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}})\right]\leq\begin{cases}c_{8}{n^{-\frac{\beta+\frac{d^{*}}{d}}{2\beta+d^{*}}}}(\log n)^{c_{9}},&amp;\text{ for }d\geq 2,\\
\\
c_{8}n^{-1/2}(\log n)^{c_{9}},&amp;\text{ for }d=1.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Here <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">W</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{X}^{\texttt{W}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the distribution of the samples generated in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is known from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oko et al., (2023)</a></cite> that diffusion model can estimate <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth densities with error rate <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n^{-(\beta+1)/(2\beta+d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-(\beta+1)/(2\beta+d)}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance. Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1 (Error bound in 𝑊_1 distance). ‣ 5.1 Convergence rate in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> establishes a rate faster than that and is free of the curse of dimensionality measured in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance. Notably, the diffusion model achieves a strictly faster rate in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance than TV distance (Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2 (Error bound in TV distance). ‣ 3.2 Convergence rate in TV distance ‣ 3 Main result ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We point out here that the sharpness of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1 (Error bound in 𝑊_1 distance). ‣ 5.1 Convergence rate in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> remains unclear. Combining the minimax lower bound for estimating <math id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-covariate <math id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth densities in <math id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang,, 2021</a>)</cite> and the simple fact that <math id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}(d^{*},d^{*},\beta,C)\subseteq\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}(d^{*},d^{*},\beta,C)\subseteq\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)</annotation></semantics></math> gives the lower bound</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\inf_{\hat{p}}\sup_{p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)}\mathds{E}_{\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\sim p_{0}}\left[W_{1}(p_{0},\hat{p})\right]\gtrsim n^{-\frac{\beta+1}{2\beta+d^{*}}}+n^{-1/2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">inf</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></munder><munder><mo lspace="0.167em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≳</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\inf_{\hat{p}}\sup_{p_{0}\in\mathcal{H}(d,d^{*},\beta,C)}\mathds{E}_{\{X_{0,i}\}_{i=1}^{n}\sim p_{0}}\left[W_{1}(p_{0},\hat{p})\right]\gtrsim n^{-\frac{\beta+1}{2\beta+d^{*}}}+n^{-1/2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a gap between the upper bound presented in Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1 (Error bound in 𝑊_1 distance). ‣ 5.1 Convergence rate in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ 5 Convergence rates in 𝑊_1 distance ‣ Optimal estimation of a factorizable density using diffusion models with ReLU neural networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> and the above lower bound when <math id="S5.Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;d^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;d^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Exploring the matching minimax optimal estimation error in this setup and whether the diffusion model can attain such an optimality calls for new techniques, and we leave it for future studies.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
